                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Dodecanoic acid was qualitatively identified in effluents from four Illinois Publicly Owned Treatment Works (POTWs)(1). Dodecanoic acid has been detected in wastewater effluents from the paint and ink industry at a concn of 434 ng/uL extract, the organics and plastics industry at a concn of 123,836 ng/uL extract, textile mills at a concn of 39 ng/uL extract, the soaps and detergents industry at a concn of 14,979 ng/uL extract, auto and other laundries at a concn of 61 ng/uL extract, pesticide manufacturing at a concn of 207 ng/uL extract, the pharmaceutical industry at a concn of 45 ng/uL extract, porcelain and enameling industry at a concn of 69 ng/uL extract, and the amusements and athletic goods industry at a concn of 1,096 ng/uL extract(2). Dodecanoic acid has been qualitatively identified in advanced waste treatment concentrates from Orange County, CA on February 3, 1976(3). Dodecanoic acid was found at a concn of 14 ng/L in wastewater from shale oil processing(4). Dodecanoic acid was identified in effluents and sludge being discharged into the Lake Michigan water basin(5). Wastewater effluents collected from the Los Angeles County waste treatment plant in 1980 and 1981 contained dodecanoic acid levels of 1,000 ug/L(6). Monitoring of effluents from a coal-fired power plant found dodecanoic acid at a concn of 80-800 ng/cu m in the vapor-phase and 90 ng/cu m in the particulate phase; 26 extracts were measured in the vapor phase and only one was measured in the particulate phase(7). Dodecanoic acid was found at a concn of 11-64 ug/L in wastewater at the Iona treatment plant in Vancouver, British Columbia(8). Dodecanoic acid was found at a maximum concn of 117 ng/L in groundwater contaminated by industrial wastes near Barcelona, Spain(9). Dodecanoic acid was identified in the emissions of a municipal waste incineration plant in Germany as having a concn of 0.94 ug/cu m(10).",
