                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Charbroiling of extra-lean and regular hamburger was found to emit 10.0-33.0 mg dodecanoic acid/kg meat(1). Frying of extra-lean and regular hamburger was found to emit 2.1 mg dodecanoic acid/kg meat; it was suggested that meat cooking operations comprises up to 21% of the total organic aerosol emissions in Los Angeles, CA(1). Dodecanoic acid was found in candle smoke from paraffin and beeswax at 0.37 and 0.49 mg/g of organic compounds(2). Dodecanoic acid was found in wood smoke from red maple(3), red oak(3), paper birch(3), white pine(3), hemlock(3), balsam fir(3), yellow poplar(4), white ash(4), sweet gum(4), mockernut hickory(4), loblolly pine(4), and slash pine(4). Dodecanoic acid was detected in wood smoke from pine, oak and synthetic logs at 1.85, 1.65 and 1.41 mg/kg of wood burnt(5). Dodecanoic acid was found in extract from a pine wood burning campfire at 450 ng/g(6). Dodecanoic acid was found at 1080 ug/g from heated roofing tar pot fumes(7). Dodecanoic acid was found in the fine aerosols from boilers burning number 2 distillate fuel oil at a rate of 1349.4 pg/kJ (burning at 58% capacity with 6.5% excess oxygen in stack gases) and a rate of 157.2 pg/kJ (burning at 54% capacity with 7.1% excess oxygen in stack gases)(8). Fine particle emission rates for dodecanoic acid from a natural gas-fired water heater and a natural gas-fired space heater were determined(9). A HEPA-filtered dilution air sample emission rate for dodecanoic acid was determined to be 6.9 pg/kJ; the emission rate for dodecanoic acid through the first filter was determined to be 120.7 pg/kJ; the emission rate for dodecanoic acid through the backup filter was 56.2 pg/kJ(9). Dodecanoic acid was found in gas and particulate matter effluents from commercial-scale meat charbroiling operations at 4,610 and 1,850 ug/kg meat cooked, respectively(10).",
